  "cerritos": "Cerritos runs a municipal water system that pulls from the Central Groundwater Basin, supplemented by imported water, to serve this well-planned community near the Orange County line. Because much of the supply is local groundwater, it arrives hard and mineral rich, treated with a disinfectant residual for safety. Residents commonly deal with scale on fixtures, cloudy spots on glassware, and the tight, dry feeling that lingers on skin and hair after a shower. Over time the minerals collect inside water heaters and dishwashers, making them less efficient. PurAgain filtration removes up to 99% of contaminants, giving families cleaner, softer water at every faucet.",
  "whittier": "Nestled against the Puente Hills in the southeast county, Whittier draws much of its water from local wells reaching into the Central Groundwater Basin, blended with imported supplies. This groundwater-heavy mix is characteristically hard and carries a disinfectant residual to protect it across the hillside neighborhoods and Uptown. Homeowners here frequently notice scale on shower glass, water spots on freshly washed dishes, and skin that feels dry after bathing. The buildup also shortens the life of water heaters and small kitchen appliances. A PurAgain reverse osmosis or whole-house conditioning system removes up to 99% of contaminants for noticeably softer, fresher water.",
  "la-mirada": "Right on the Los Angeles and Orange County line, La Mirada receives water largely drawn from the Central Groundwater Basin and delivered by the local water utility serving this southeast suburb. Groundwater from this basin runs hard and is treated with a disinfectant residual before it reaches homes near Biola University and the 5 freeway. That hardness shows up as scale on fixtures, filmy residue on glassware, and dry skin and hair after a shower. The minerals also build up inside appliances and plumbing, cutting their lifespan short. With a PurAgain system removing up to 99% of contaminants, your household water becomes cleaner, softer, and better tasting.",
  "pasadena": "Pasadena Water and Power supplies the city at the base of the San Gabriel Mountains, blending imported water with groundwater pumped from the local Raymond Basin beneath the foothills. Both sources contribute to water that runs distinctly hard, and imported deliveries typically carry a chloramine disinfectant that persists through the system. For homeowners from Old Town to the Arroyo, that hardness means scale on fixtures, spots drying onto dishes, and skin and hair that feel dry after showering. The dissolved minerals steadily build up inside water heaters and appliances, shortening their useful life. A PurAgain reverse osmosis or whole-house system removes up to 99% of contaminants, giving families the clean, soft water this historic city calls for.",
